{
 "engine": "Groq whisper-large-v3",
 "text": "Gladiator fights didn't start as entertainment. They started at a funeral. 264 BC, Rome. A powerful man, Brutus Pera, has died. To honor him, his sons hold something new in the city's cattle market, armed men fighting in pairs. The Romans called it a munus, a duty owed to the dead. The fighters were likely prisoners of war. Soon, rich families compete with bigger funeral games. One funeral has 22 pairs. The games become a way to win the crowd. Centuries later, emperors fill giant arenas like the Colosseum, a funeral ritual that became Rome's most famous show. Every legend has a beginning.",
